Frame 0 of a MULTI-FRAME encode diverges from C at presets 2-7 — 2026-09-10

A key frame that is byte-identical to C when encoded alone stops being
byte-identical when a second frame follows it. Presets 2-7 only.

REPRODUCER (minimal)
  tools/identity_diff_inter.sh 256 256 40 6 2 rawseq:johnny_256x256_8f.i420
  with SVTAV1_INTER_EXPERIMENTAL=1 and SVTAV1_FRAME_SHIFT/_ZOOM_* unset.
  Asset: R2 video/pd-derf-720p/johnny_256x256_8f.i420

PRESET BRACKET (johnny 256x256, cli_qp 40, frames=2, frame 0 vs C)
  p0   OK
  p2   DIFF   C=4857  rs=4824
  p4   DIFF   C=4870  rs=4870     <- same byte COUNT, different bytes
  p5   DIFF   C=4919  rs=4894
  p6   DIFF   C=5053  rs=5048
  p7   DIFF   C=5254  rs=5232
  p8   OK
  p10  OK
Clean at the slowest preset AND at the fast ones; dirty only in the middle.

THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THIS A FINDING. Same pixels, same preset, frames=1:
byte-identical on all six cells that fail at frames=2 (fourpeople_256,
johnny_256, kristenandsara_128, kristenandsara_256, vidyo3_256, vidyo4_256).
So it is not the content, not the crop size, and not the still path.

RULED OUT — the harness. identity_diff_inter.sh hands the two encoders
different GOP settings (C: SVT_INTRA_PERIOD=-1; port: SVTAV1_INTRA_PERIOD=64),
which is exactly the kind of mismatch that would invalidate the result. It does
not explain it: matching them in either direction, and setting both to 255,
leaves the bytes UNCHANGED at 5053/5048.

  asis (C=-1, port=64)      DIFF  C=5053 rs=5048
  SVT_INTRA_PERIOD=64       DIFF  C=5053 rs=5048
  SVTAV1_INTRA_PERIOD=-1    DIFF  C=5053 rs=5048
  both=255                  DIFF  C=5053 rs=5048

SEPARATE from the 180/180 stills result. real_image_matrix.sh drives
identity_diff.sh, which passes NO GOP configuration at all. CID22 1001682 at
512x512 q40 p6 through that harness is identical at 10720 B, and stays identical
here at frames=1.

LEAD, not yet tested. identity_run.rs records that `recon_output` reaches the
encode through `postfilter_consumed` (pipeline.rs), "which a multi-frame run
already forces true via `!is_single_frame` — a later frame may predict from this
recon, so the filters run either way". So frame 0's post-filters (deblock / CDEF
/ restoration) RUN in a multi-frame encode and are skipped in a single-frame one.
If the port's filter DECISIONS differ from C's in that mode, frame 0 diverges,
and the p0-clean / p8-clean bracket would point at a mid-preset search shortcut
rather than the filters themselves. Test that before assuming it.

METHOD NOTE, because it nearly produced a wrong answer. At frames=1 this harness
writes c.obu / rs.obu; only a multi-frame run writes the per-frame c.obu.pts<N> /
rs.obu.f<N>. A first pass compared the per-frame names at frames=1, found them
missing, and `cmp -s` on two absent files reports "differ" — which read as a
divergence on every cell and pointed at content rather than the GOP, the exact
opposite of the truth. Check that the artifacts exist before comparing them.
